A condensation counter grows particles to detect them (@cm-cpc-response: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP", #source "../sources/Mobility-Particle-Size-Spectrometers-Wiedensohler.lingua#r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM", #source "../sources/CPC-Proportionality-Calibration-Owen-Mulholland-Guthrie.lingua#r_7XKKJ1Q7HNDS2BEPSHZDJMTKVE", #clean-manufacturing "Coincidence-models-have-different-inverses.lingua#r_2MAE4G406EHQN1WZRJTBHQVGH0") { r_35HXRPRA72SS0K0CJQH7N1TPGD
**Read:** [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP), section 21.7.2; [Mobility particle size spectrometers — Wiedensohler and colleagues](../sources/Mobility-Particle-Size-Spectrometers-Wiedensohler.lingua#r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM), section 4.2; [CPC proportionality calibration — Owen, Mulholland and Guthrie](../sources/CPC-Proportionality-Calibration-Owen-Mulholland-Guthrie.lingua#r_7XKKJ1Q7HNDS2BEPSHZDJMTKVE), introduction and apparatus.

A condensation particle counter creates conditions in which vapor can condense on particles and grow them into optically detectable droplets. The detected droplet is part of the measuring process. Its final optical size does not directly report the original particle's diameter.

Detection includes transport to the growth region, activation, sufficient growth and optical/electronic registration. The efficiency can depend on initial diameter and composition as well as operating conditions. The diameter where efficiency is half a defined plateau is a property of that response curve, not a hard physical boundary below which no particles exist.

For a two-population teaching sample with concentrations 100 and 200 cm⁻³ and detection probabilities 0.2 and 0.95, an otherwise lossless counter expects 210 cm⁻³ of detected events, not 300. Dividing by one arbitrarily chosen average efficiency can hide the missing population. A mobility classifier plus counter can provide size-dependent information, but introduces its own transmission and inversion.

**Work:** Draw an efficiency curve and predict responses for populations concentrated below, near and above its transition. Separate an efficiency calibration versus size from a linearity test versus concentration. Identify what flow, temperature, working-fluid and background information is needed to interpret a changed count. Do not apply a reference aerosol's behavior to an unknown material without examining that transfer.

